#. [lua]: when
#: lua/sw_tips.lua:43
msgid "Weapon ranges"
msgstr ""

#. [lua]: when
#: lua/sw_tips.lua:44
msgid "Blasters reach beyond the next hex: a rifle hits 2 hexes away, heavy weapons 3, with -10% to hit per hex beyond the first. A defender only shoots back if its weapon reaches that far, so keep your riflemen at range from pistols and blades.\n\nRight-click any unit and choose <b>Show attack range</b> to see where it can strike. Rifles also offer an <b>aimed shot</b>: one shot fewer, +20% to hit."
msgstr ""

#. [lua]: when
#: lua/sw_tips.lua:48
msgid "Your Force user carries Force Points (blue pips) and regains a few each turn. Right-click them and choose <b>The Force…</b>: <b>Push</b> and <b>Pull</b> move enemies (into walls for damage), <b>Speed</b> gives extra moves, <b>Sense</b> reveals hidden enemies, and <b>Mind Trick</b> stops a weak-minded enemy from acting."
msgstr ""

#. [lua]: when
#. [message]: speaker=narrator
#: lua/sw_tips.lua:51
#: utils/hte_macros.cfg:479
msgid "Overwatch"
msgstr ""

#. [lua]: when
#: lua/sw_tips.lua:52
msgid "A soldier that could still attack but ends its turn without attacking goes on <b>overwatch</b>: until your next turn it fires on an enemy that moves into range in plain sight. Holding a line of riflemen back is often stronger than charging. Choose <b>Overwatch</b> from the right-click menu to take it before moving the rest of your force."
msgstr ""

#. [lua]: when
#. [message]: speaker=narrator
#: lua/sw_tips.lua:61
#: utils/hte_macros.cfg:581
msgid "Flanking"
msgstr ""

#. [lua]: when
#: lua/sw_tips.lua:62
msgid "Surround before you strike. Every one of your units standing on the far side of an enemy -- away from the attacker -- adds to the attack. Pin an enemy from the front, then hit it from behind. The enemy flanks too: do not let a hero be surrounded."
msgstr ""

#. [lua]: when
#: lua/sw_tips.lua:69
msgid "Sensor contacts"
msgstr ""

#. [lua]: when
#: lua/sw_tips.lua:70
msgid "The amber hexes are sensor contacts: enemies your sensors detect but your eyes cannot see. A question mark is an unknown contact; a symbol shows what it is. Some are decoys. Units with active sensors can make an <b>Active sensor sweep</b> instead of attacking to see further and identify contacts."
msgstr ""

#. [lua]: when
#: lua/sw_tips.lua:77
msgid "You have starfighter sorties overhead. Right-click a hex one of your forward observers can see and call a <b>strafing run</b> (strikes at once along a line) or a <b>bombing run</b> (lands next turn on the marked hexes -- clear your own troops out). Each call uses the observer's attack."
msgstr ""

#. [lua]: when
#: lua/sw_tips.lua:83
msgid "Getting in unseen"
msgstr ""

#. [lua]: when
#: lua/sw_tips.lua:84
msgid "The guards here are unaware. Their sight is shown around them; a guard that spots you shouts and alerts every guard near it. A guard sees only the way it faces. Sneak up from behind, out of its sight, and right-click your unit next to it for a <b>Silent takedown</b> in the same turn: guards look around at the start of their turn and notice anyone beside them. Fighting openly is possible, but the whole garrison will come."
msgstr ""

#. [lua]: when
#. [label]
#: lua/sw_tips.lua:89
#: scenarios/heir_to_the_empire/01_ysalamiri_harvest.cfg:199
msgid "Ysalamiri"
msgstr ""

#. [lua]: when
#: lua/sw_tips.lua:90
msgid "The violet-tinted hexes are a ysalamiri field: the creatures push back the Force. A Force user inside has no powers, no blaster deflection and no Force regeneration. Fight around the field, or send troops in instead."
msgstr ""

#. [lua]: when
#: lua/sw_tips.lua:98
msgid "Grand Admiral Thrawn studies you. His forces gain Insight from how you fight -- the tactics you repeat, the weapons and targets you favour -- and as it grows they predict your moves and counter your sensors. Vary your approach and he learns slower."
msgstr ""

#. [lua]: when
#. [message]: speaker=narrator
#: lua/sw_tips.lua:108
#: utils/hte_macros.cfg:592
msgid "Ranks"
msgstr ""

#. [lua]: when
#: lua/sw_tips.lua:109
msgid "A unit past its highest level now earns ranks -- Veteran, Seasoned veteran, Elite -- shown as insignia. Every rank also brings a new ability for the unit's class, listed at the end of its description. Many units and heroes also change their look as Seasoned veterans and Elites. At each rank, most units choose their training too: more hit points, a weapons drill or a mobility drill. Keep your veterans alive: they carry over to the next mission."
msgstr ""

