#. [message]: speaker=narrator
#: utils/hte_macros.cfg:472
msgid "The Force and ysalamiri"
msgstr ""

#. [message]: speaker=narrator
#: utils/hte_macros.cfg:472
msgid ""
"Force users carry Force Points (FP), shown as blue pips beside them. They regain a few each turn. Right-click a Force user and choose <b>The Force…</b>. Every power is listed with what it does, its FP cost, its range, how long it takes to recover and whether it uses your attack. Powers you cannot use right now are greyed out with the reason.\n"
"\n"
"<b>Force Push</b> (2 FP, range 2, uses your attack): hurls an enemy one hex straight away from you. <b>Force Pull</b> (2 FP, range 3, uses your attack): drags an enemy at least two hexes away one hex toward you. When you pick a target, an arrow on the map shows which way it will go. If a unit, a wall or the edge of the battlefield is in the way, bold text warns that the pushed enemy will slam into it for 6 impact damage and stay where it is.\n"
"\n"
"<b>Force Speed</b> (2 FP, once per turn): 2 extra movement points this turn. <b>Force Sense</b> (1 FP, once per turn): reveals every living enemy within 4 hexes until your next turn, even in fog or deep forest, and strips hiding creatures such as Noghri of their stealth. Living presences up to 8 hexes away are marked as unknown contacts. Droids, technological cloaks and anything inside a ysalamiri field cannot be sensed. <b>Mind Trick</b> (3 FP, range 2): a weak-minded enemy of level 2 or below cannot move or attack on its next turn; leaders and Force users resist. <b>Force Choke</b> (3 FP, range 3): 10 arcane damage, dark side only. <b>Blaster Deflection</b> works by itself: it turns aside an overwatch volley for 1 FP.\n"
"\n"
"A ysalamiri pushes the Force back in a bubble around itself, drawn as a faint violet tint with a violet outline at its edge. A Force user inside shows a violet slashed ring: no powers, no deflection, no regeneration, and no Force agility or healing. Nothing inside can be targeted by a Force power either. A carried ysalamiri moves its bubble with its bearer. On Myrkr, the ysalamiri homeworld, the whole planet is such a field and no one can touch the Force."
msgstr ""

#. [message]: speaker=narrator
#: utils/hte_macros.cfg:479
msgid ""
"A soldier that could still attack but ends its turn without attacking goes on <b>overwatch</b> automatically. Units that cannot attack this turn (they attacked, used their action, are dazed or unaware) do not. To take it earlier, right-click the soldier and choose <b>Overwatch</b>: it gives up this turn's attack and stops moving. A gold crosshair marks the unit, with one bar per reaction left. Until its next turn, an enemy that moves within its range and in plain sight draws reaction fire. The moving unit stops when fired on but keeps its remaining moves.\n"
"\n"
"Hidden units cannot be targeted. If one of your watchers is itself hidden from the enemy (cloaked, in stealth, or unseen), you are asked before it fires: opening fire reveals it until its next turn, and holding fire keeps it hidden without spending the reaction. Each watcher fires at most once per enemy move order. Overwatch ends at the start of its side's next turn. Enemy units go on overwatch the same way when they end a turn without attacking."
msgstr ""

#. [message]: speaker=narrator
#: utils/hte_macros.cfg:482
msgid "Blaster fire"
msgstr ""

#. [message]: speaker=narrator
#: utils/hte_macros.cfg:482
msgid ""
"The colour of a bolt tells you who is shooting: red for the Imperial Remnant and other dark forces, blue or green for the New Republic and its allies, orange for independent smugglers.\n"
"\n"
"Some craft keep their own colours: TIE starfighters and Star Destroyer turbolasers fire green, and X-wing, Y-wing and A-wing lasers fire red. Stun settings fire blue rings, and ion cannons fire a pale blue discharge that slows what it hits. The muzzle flash when a unit fires is always the colour of its shot."
msgstr ""

#. [message]: speaker=narrator
#: utils/hte_macros.cfg:485
msgid "Space battles"
msgstr ""

#. [message]: speaker=narrator
#: utils/hte_macros.cfg:485
msgid ""
"Open space can only be crossed by starfighters and capital ships; ground troops stay aboard. Starships cannot be recalled into a ground battle, and ground troops cannot be recalled into a space battle; they rejoin you after the mission.\n"
"\n"
"<b>Evasion.</b> Every craft has an evasion rating, shown as an ability: its chance to dodge a shot in open space. Asteroid fields add 10% (up to 70%) and station decks take 10% away. A-wings and TIE Interceptors dodge 65%, TIE fighters 55%, X-wings 50% (Luke's and Wedge's 60%), Y-wings and TIE bombers 35%, boarding shuttles 25%, and capital ships only 10%. A marksman shot (proton torpedoes, concussion missiles) still hits at least 60% of the time, so save it for an evasive target.\n"
"\n"
"<b>Roles.</b> Each craft has a job, described in its unit help:\n"
"• <b>X-wing</b> — multirole line fighter. Shields and an astromech droid (repairs 4 HP a turn) keep it in a long fight.\n"
"• <b>A-wing</b> — interceptor. The fastest and most evasive craft you have. It ignores enemy zones of control, so it can slip past escorts to catch bombers and shuttles. It carries concussion missiles and jams enemy sensors, but it is fragile once hit.\n"
"• <b>Y-wing</b> — assault bomber. It is slow, armoured and easy to hit. Its anti-ship torpedoes deal +50% damage to capital ships and installations, and its ion cannon slows its target. Escort it.\n"
"• <b>TIE fighter</b> — cheap swarm fighter. It is nimble, unshielded and dangerous in packs.\n"
"• <b>TIE Interceptor</b> — elite dogfighter. It is fast and evasive, and its four cannons strike first.\n"
"• <b>TIE bomber</b> — anti-ship bomber. Its unguided bombs do +50% damage to warships but only half damage to anything that can dodge. Kill it before it reaches your warships.\n"
"\n"
"Proton torpedoes and concussion missiles are limited: an X-wing has 2 volleys, a Y-wing 3 and an A-wing 2 missile volleys per mission. Every fight the ordnance is used in spends one volley, attacking or firing back, and the magazines are refilled between missions. Asteroid fields cost starfighters extra movement but give them cover, and capital ships cannot enter them.\n"
"\n"
"New squadrons launch from hangar bays and launch rails, which work like a keep and castle."
msgstr ""

#. [message]: speaker=narrator
#: utils/hte_macros.cfg:500
msgid "Bases and settlements"
msgstr ""

#. [message]: speaker=narrator
#: utils/hte_macros.cfg:500
msgid ""
"Command bunkers and shuttle landing pads are keeps: a leader standing on one can call in reinforcements onto the surrounding bunker aprons or landing aprons.\n"
"\n"
"Settlements, supply depots, Wookiee dwellings, native villages, Noghri dukhas, farmsteads, hunters' outposts and Imperial field camps count as villages: they heal units and earn credits each turn for whoever holds them. Each kind looks different from planet to planet and from hex to hex, but they all work the same.\n"
"\n"
"Buildings have their own floors and walls: timber lodges, duracrete colony buildings, palace halls and tunnels cut into rock, as well as starship decks. Floors are open ground. Walls and bulkheads cannot be crossed and block a guard's line of sight. <b>Cargo crates</b> are cover: a unit behind them gets castle-level defence, or the floor's if that is better. Forests, settlements and crates also make intruders harder for guards to spot.\n"
"\n"
"<b>Aboard the Katana</b>, the central spine is the shortest route to the bridge. The northern maintenance passage and southern cargo bays connect the supply rooms and approach the bridge from the sides. Supply rooms heal; ordinary cargo crates do not. Side passages also connect to the Imperial boarding shafts, so secure the junctions before the announced reinforcements arrive.\n"
"\n"
"<b>The Cloning Vats</b> has eight production bays around a central dispatch aisle. The aisle offers the quickest advance; staggered bay doors and hewn service passages along both sides let you split your assault between cylinders. Equipment crates provide cover, but no healing. The eastern passage also connects to the clone reinforcement entry: secure its junctions and keep Han protected while other squads clear the bays.\n"
"\n"
"<b>Honoghr's Choice</b> centres on the timber clan house at Nystao. Its west, east, north and south entrances open onto village tracks through Honoghr's poisoned soil. Broken earthworks offer positions outside the house, while supply crates provide cover without healing. The surrounding dukhas heal and earn credits. Keep a reserve near Leia: Imperial troops approach from the west, and the announced eastern shuttle can contest the village paths behind your defenders.\n"
"\n"
"<b>The Bilbringi Shipyards</b> has four docking piers joined by a narrow service spine. Open space and channels through the visible asteroid formations let capital ships approach the docks. Fighters can instead enter those formations for better cover at a higher movement cost. Docking decks offer less fighter cover than open space. Visible asteroid terrain adds no mine damage: the existing cloaked asteroid screen remains a separate hazard, and central or outer routes can still bring ships close to it. You need to destroy three of the four platforms.\n"
"\n"
"<b>Nomad City</b> has mining bays linked by service doors and an open central aisle. Hold the mining bays to defend the five miner sheds, keep a reserve in the aisle, or intercept thieves near the city gates. Cargo crates provide cover without healing; the benefit depends on the unit, so check its defence before committing. The sheds still heal. Outside the city, exposed desert and dunes inflict heat damage at the end of your side's turn: plan routes through the existing shade and protect Lando.\n"
"\n"
"<b>Peregrine's Nest</b> has a central canyon and two longer outer trails leading to the side gates. Sheer ridges and hewn walls block movement. Hold the canyon or watch the flanks, and keep reserves near the durasteel generator room. Cargo crates offer cover without healing; check each unit's defence before choosing a position. The six original supply depots still heal. Any Imperial unit reaching the shield generator ends the mission, and Han must survive.\n"
"\n"
"<b>Bpfassh</b> has covered passages through three colony buildings and cargo crates along the streets. Screen Leia through the southern passages, regroup near the militia's Assembly Plaza, then choose an east-district passage or an existing street toward the Falcon. Crates provide cover without healing or recruitment; check each unit's defence. Parks still conceal Noghri, so scout ahead and protect Leia: her death ends the mission. Both the passages and the open roads can be contested."
msgstr ""

#. [message]: speaker=narrator
#: utils/hte_macros.cfg:519
msgid "Heroes"
msgstr ""

#. [message]: speaker=narrator
#: utils/hte_macros.cfg:519
msgid ""
"Named heroes cannot be replaced. Each mission names its critical heroes in the objectives: if one of them falls, the mission is lost.\n"
"\n"
"Other heroes who fall are not killed. They withdraw from the fight badly wounded, marked <i>withdrawn</i>, and are gone for the rest of the mission along with their firepower. They return, healed, for the next mission. The objectives list who withdraws. Heroes who survive also continue to the next mission."
msgstr ""

#. [message]: speaker=narrator
#: utils/hte_macros.cfg:522
msgid "Stealth and hidden hazards"
msgstr ""

#. [message]: speaker=narrator
#: utils/hte_macros.cfg:522
msgid ""
"Noghri commandos are invisible in forest or at night, unless an enemy with <i>keen senses</i> is within two hexes of them. They are revealed when an enemy moves next to them.\n"
"\n"
"Cloaked asteroids stay hidden until your sensors identify them or something runs into them, and a ship that ends its turn next to one takes damage, though never enough to destroy it. Sensors cannot track living stealth such as the Noghri's; Force Sense and keen senses can."
msgstr ""

#. [message]: speaker=narrator
#: utils/hte_macros.cfg:525
msgid "Infiltration and guards"
msgstr ""

#. [message]: speaker=narrator
#: utils/hte_macros.cfg:525
msgid ""
"Some missions are about getting in unseen. Guards start <b>unaware</b>: they hold their posts or walk a short patrol and do not attack or give chase. A guard spots your unit if it has a clear line of sight to it within 3 hexes (4 for vornskrs). Cover (forest, crates, settlements or any hex giving 50% defence or more) and night each cut that by 1. Fighting this turn makes noise and adds 1. A unit next to a guard is always seen. Walls block sight.\n"
"\n"
"A guard that spots you becomes <b>alert</b> (\"!\") and shouts, alerting every guard within 4 hexes. Alert guards fight normally. A guard just beyond seeing distance shows \"?\". Some actions, such as taking a prize the guards protect, raise the alarm for everyone.\n"
"\n"
"A guard sees only the half of the map it faces, the way its sprite looks; vornskrs sense all round. Step next to an unaware guard from behind, right-click your unit and choose <b>Silent takedown</b> to knock the guard out with your attack. Do it in the same turn: at the start of their turn guards on post turn about, patrols face the way they walk, and any guard notices an intruder standing beside it. Any other unaware guard who can see the spot is alerted. Vornskrs cannot be taken down. Amber-tinted hexes with an amber rim show what each unaware guard can see right now. They are only visible to you. A guard's hexes disappear the moment it is alerted or falls. Choose <b>Show or hide guard sight</b> to turn them off or back on."
msgstr ""

#. [message]: speaker=narrator
#: utils/hte_macros.cfg:530
msgid "Sensors and cloaking"
msgstr ""

#. [message]: speaker=narrator
#: utils/hte_macros.cfg:530
msgid ""
"Your sensors build a picture of enemies you cannot see. An amber dashed hex with a question mark is an unknown contact. An amber hex with a symbol is classified by type: capital ship (wedge), starfighter (X), vehicle, infantry, creature, or object. A red hex with corner brackets is identified but out of sight. Red brackets around a visible ship mean you have burned through its cloak. Right-click a marked hex and choose <b>Sensor contact</b> to see the strength of the reading and what it is made of.\n"
"\n"
"Sensor strength is the observer's sensors, minus 1 for every 2 hexes of distance, minus enemy jamming, plus the target's signature, minus its cloak and cover. Asteroid fields, forests, settlements and mountains give cover. A reading of 0 is a contact, 3 classifies it, and 6 identifies it. Being next to a unit always identifies it. A cloaked unit stays hidden until your team identifies it, and its cloak drops for a turn when it fires.\n"
"\n"
"Units with active sensors can make an <b>Active sensor sweep</b> instead of attacking: until their next turn they add their sweep rating and see 2 hexes further, but they become easier to detect. Jammers (ECM, yellow waves) weaken enemy sensors near them and near what they protect, but are loud themselves; counter-jamming (ECCM) cancels jamming. Some ships launch <b>sensor decoys</b> that read as real contacts until enemy sensors see through them or a unit comes next to them. Treat unconfirmed contacts with suspicion.\n"
"\n"
"Enemy AI can make one sweep per side per turn, before combat when an unidentified contact is within a scanner's boosted range, or with a spare attack afterward. It can also spend one unit's remaining attack and decoy charge to place a false contact toward a known threat. Sweeping makes the scanner easier to detect; close identification, stronger sensors and your own sweeps expose decoys."
msgstr ""

#. [message]: speaker=narrator
#: utils/hte_macros.cfg:537
msgid "Electronic warfare"
msgstr ""

#. [message]: speaker=narrator
#: utils/hte_macros.cfg:537
msgid ""
"Electronic warfare (EW) decides who can find whom on sensors. Every unit has EW ratings. A unit's <b>sensors and EW</b> ability lists only the ratings that differ from an ordinary unit of its kind, and says what they mean.\n"
"\n"
"<b>Sensors</b>: how strongly and how far the unit detects enemies it cannot see. A soldier has 2 out to 3 hexes, a vehicle 3 out to 4, a starfighter 3 out to 5, a capital ship 5 out to 7.\n"
"\n"
"<b>Signature</b>: how loud the unit is on enemy sensors. Each point is worth about 2 hexes of detection range. A soldier is 2, a starfighter 3, a vehicle 4, a capital ship 8. Commandos, Noghri and infiltrators <i>run silent</i> at 1. Fighting adds 2 until the unit's next turn, a sweep adds 3, and a working jammer adds 2.\n"
"\n"
"<b>Cloak</b>: subtracts from the signature, and the unit stays hidden until an enemy team identifies it or a unit comes next to it. Fighting drops the cloak until the unit's next turn.\n"
"\n"
"<b>Active sensor sweep</b> (right-click; uses the attack): until the unit's next turn its sensors gain its sweep rating and reach 2 hexes further, but its signature rises by 3.\n"
"\n"
"<b>Jammer</b> (ECM, yellow waves): enemy sensors read weaker by the jammer's rating when they look from, or at, a hex within the jammer's range. Only the strongest jammer counts. <b>Counter-jamming</b> (ECCM) cancels that many points of jamming on the unit's own sensors.\n"
"\n"
"<b>Sensor decoy</b> (right-click; uses the attack; limited charges): a false contact up to 2 hexes away for 2 turns. A sweep, strong sensors or a unit moving next to it gives it away.\n"
"\n"
"<b>Cover</b>: a unit in forest, a settlement or mountains reads 1 weaker, and in an asteroid field 2 weaker. An observer inside an asteroid field also sees 1 worse.\n"
"\n"
"<b>Reading it</b>: sensors − 1 per 2 hexes of distance − jamming + signature − cloak − cover. At 0 or more the enemy has an unknown contact, at 3 it knows the type, at 6 it has identified the unit. Being next to a unit always identifies it. Example: a scout trooper (sensors 3) looks at a New Republic Commando (signature 1) 4 hexes away in forest: 3 − 2 + 1 − 1 = 1, an unknown contact. If the commando fires this turn, the reading rises to 3 and the enemy knows it is infantry. Beyond the scout's 5-hex reach it does not register at all."
msgstr ""

#. [message]: speaker=narrator
#: utils/hte_macros.cfg:554
msgid "Smuggler specialists"
msgstr ""

#. [message]: speaker=narrator
#: utils/hte_macros.cfg:554
msgid ""
"Recruit a <b>Smuggler Slicer</b> in The Smugglers' Council when reconnaissance matters more than another gunhand. A slicer costs 18 gold: 28 HP, 6 movement, a 5×2 blaster pistol and a 4×2 vibroknife. The pistol only reaches the adjacent hex, so keep the specialist behind armed support.\n"
"\n"
"Passive sensors have strength 3 out to 5 hexes. Counter-jamming 1 cancels one point of interference for that observer; contacts are shared with the team. An <b>Active sensor sweep</b> spends the attack to raise sensors to 5 and reach to 7 until the next turn, while making the slicer easier to detect. A <b>sensor decoy</b> also spends the attack and one of two finite charges. Both leave ordinary movement available, but a slicer cannot sweep, deploy a decoy and fire in the same turn. Sweeps and close identification expose enemy false contacts."
msgstr ""

#. [message]: speaker=narrator
#: utils/hte_macros.cfg:557
msgid "Wookiee support fire"
msgstr ""

#. [message]: speaker=narrator
#: utils/hte_macros.cfg:557
msgid ""
"Recruit a <b>Wookiee Suppressor</b> in Shadows of Kashyyyk to disrupt an advance. It costs 22 gold: 34 HP, 5 movement, a 4×2 ryyk blade and a 4×2 concussive bowcaster reaching 2 hexes. At two hexes it can also aim: 4×1 with +20% accuracy before distance penalties.\n"
"\n"
"A bowcaster hit in <b>ordinary combat</b>, including return fire, slows the enemy until that enemy ends a turn. Slow halves its attack damage and doubles its movement costs. A miss does nothing; the attack still spends your action. Slowing limits pursuit, but does not stop an enemy from moving or attacking. Use cover and armed support, and check the attack forecast before relying on a hit.\n"
"\n"
"Suppressors give up a warrior's durability and damage, and have no lookout keen senses. Pair them with lookouts against hidden Noghri and warriors against a close assault. <b>Overwatch volleys deal damage only and do not apply Slow</b>: fire now to try to disrupt the enemy, or hold your attack for reaction fire.\n"
"\n"
"Keeping a Suppressor alive earns <b>thick hide</b> at Veteran (positive resistances doubled while defending, capped at 50%; vulnerabilities stay), <b>covering fire</b> at Seasoned veteran (bowcaster first strike, including normal return fire), and <b>fire discipline</b> at Elite (adjacent allies gain +10% ranged accuracy, without stacking). These ranks add no attacks, and the aimed shot still cannot return fire."
msgstr ""

#. [message]: speaker=narrator
#: utils/hte_macros.cfg:564
msgid "Wayland field medicine"
msgstr ""

#. [message]: speaker=narrator
#: utils/hte_macros.cfg:564
msgid ""
"Recruit a <b>Myneyrshi Field Medic</b> at The Gates of Tantiss to keep a forest assault moving. For 20 gold it has 28 HP, 6 movement and weak 4×2 spear and bow attacks. It crosses forest for <b>1 movement point</b> per hex; the Republic Field Medic pays 2 and moves 5. Both pay 2 on hills. Myneyrshi medics take 10% extra fire damage, so keep warriors between them and blasters.\n"
"\n"
"At the <b>start of the medic's turn</b>, adjacent allies recover up to 4 HP. A poisoned ally takes no poison damage but receives no healing and stays poisoned. The medic does not heal itself, and multiple healers do not add their healing together. Move beside a wounded unit before ending your turn; passive care leaves normal movement and attacks available.\n"
"\n"
"Rank I grants <b>Forest ghost</b>, concealment in forest unless an enemy is adjacent. Rank II grants <b>Field surgery</b>: one healing +8 ability replaces healing +4. Rank III grants <b>Rapid evacuation</b>, ignoring enemy zones of control. These ranks add no attacks. Early Republic medic ranks reach stronger healing sooner; the Myneyrshi option trades that early treatment and durability for forest mobility. The shared Warrior artwork is reused; check the unit name and healing ability when selecting support troops."
msgstr ""

#. [message]: speaker=narrator
#: utils/hte_macros.cfg:569
msgid "Weapon ranges and aimed shots"
msgstr ""

#. [message]: speaker=narrator
#: utils/hte_macros.cfg:569
msgid ""
"Ranged weapons reach beyond the next hex. Each weapon shows its reach as a special, such as <i>range 2</i>, beside its damage and strikes. Pistols and other close weapons reach only adjacent targets. Rifles, carbines, bowcasters and Han Solo's DL-44 reach 2 hexes. A Jedi's blaster deflection turns bolts back at a shooter up to 2 hexes away (it only answers fire, never starts it). E-Web repeaters, AT-ST cannons, guided torpedoes and capital-ship batteries reach 3. Every hex beyond the first costs 10% chance to hit, except for guided torpedoes.\n"
"\n"
"To attack from range, select your unit and click an enemy within reach. A defender can only shoot back if its own weapon reaches that far, so a rifleman two hexes from a pistol-armed enemy fires without reply.\n"
"\n"
"Rifles, carbines, bowcasters, bows and the DL-44 also offer an <b>aimed shot</b>: one shot fewer, but +20% to hit. Aiming takes a moment, so it needs at least two hexes of distance and only works when you attack. Choose it in the attack dialog when the target is in cover or far away.\n"
"\n"
"To see a unit's reach, right-click it (yours or an enemy you can see) and choose <b>Show attack range</b>. Solid markers show the hexes it can hit from where it stands. Dotted markers show the hexes it could hit after moving: with the moves it has left for your own units, a full move for anyone else's. The markers clear when that unit moves, at the start of the next turn, or with <b>Hide attack range</b>. Enemy riflemen and gunners use their range too, firing from a distance your weapons may not reach."
msgstr ""

#. [message]: speaker=narrator
#: utils/hte_macros.cfg:576
msgid "Knife attacks and backstab"
msgstr ""

#. [message]: speaker=narrator
#: utils/hte_macros.cfg:576
msgid ""
"Noghri commandos, Khabarakh and Imperial infiltrators can double their knife damage by attacking a target with a unit hostile to it on the exact opposite hex. An empty hex, an ally of the target or a petrified unit gives no backstab bonus. Backstab never doubles return-fire damage.\n"
"\n"
"Backstab can use an unarmed supporting unit. The separate flanking accuracy bonus needs armed support on the far side; it remains capped at +20%. Inspect the attack dialog before committing, and protect the opposite hex when defending against enemy knives.\n"
"\n"
"Units already stored in an older save may retain their saved weapon specials; newly created units use the current definitions."
msgstr ""

#. [message]: speaker=narrator
#: utils/hte_macros.cfg:581
msgid ""
"Surround an enemy before you strike. When you attack, every one of your units (or your allies') standing next to the target on its <b>far side</b>, away from the attacker, gives +10% chance to hit, up to +20%. For a melee attack these are the three hexes directly behind the target; for a shot from range, the hexes behind it along the line of fire. The attack dialog shows <i>flanking +10%</i> or <i>flanking +20%</i> when it applies.\n"
"\n"
"Only armed units flank, and flanking helps only the attacker, never return fire. Units beside the attacker do not count. The enemy flanks too, so keep your flanks covered and don't let a unit be surrounded."
msgstr ""

#. [message]: speaker=narrator
#: utils/hte_macros.cfg:584
msgid "Enemy tactics"
msgstr ""

#. [message]: speaker=narrator
#: utils/hte_macros.cfg:584
msgid ""
"Enemy riflemen slightly prefer wounded targets and troops without nearby armed support when choosing among worthwhile shots. These preferences never add damage or accuracy. Keep wounded soldiers covered and close to supporting units; enemies cannot plan around troops they cannot see.\n"
"\n"
"When choosing a shot after moving, enemy riflemen look for an approach outside visible overwatch lanes. Reaction fire interrupts a move even when it misses. Watchers that are hidden, out of reactions or disabled do not deter this planner, and a soldier already in position can still shoot. Cover several approaches to deny easy firing positions; watch for a flank instead of assuming an enemy will walk into your crosshair. This caution uses visible firing lanes rather than knowledge of your hidden sensors. Ordinary enemy movement can still enter overwatch when no safe ranged shot is available.\n"
"\n"
"An ordinary enemy unit at 40% health or less may withdraw before combat if it can get at least two hexes farther from the nearest visible threat and beyond every visible enemy's current weapon reach. It favors cover and nearby allies. Withdrawing uses normal movement, obeys zones of control, and gives up its remaining movement and attack for that turn. Leaders and unaware guards keep their posts. Block escape routes or pursue a withdrawal with covered troops; the retreat grants no healing or extra movement."
msgstr ""

#. [message]: speaker=narrator
#: utils/hte_macros.cfg:589
msgid ""
"Starfighters support ground battles from off the map. When your side has sorties, right-click a hex that one of your forward observers can see (or tracks on sensors) within range and choose <b>Call air support</b>. Forward observers are commanders such as Han, Leia and Lando, sergeants, commandos and scouts; calling uses the observer's attack, and only one sortie flies per turn.\n"
"\n"
"A <b>strafing run</b> strikes at once along four hexes leading away from the observer: three shots of 6 at every unit there. A <b>bombing run</b> marks the target and the six hexes around it with orange circles and lands at the start of your next turn: two bombs of 9 at every unit there, with +10% accuracy. Sorties hit friend and foe alike, so mind your own troops. Each shot's chance to hit depends on the target's terrain cover; enemy anti-air within range lowers accuracy by 20% each (40% at most), and enemy jamming that reaches the selected strike hex (the center of a bombing run) lowers it by 10% per point, after the observer's counter-jamming. Anti-air protects any struck hex; jamming is measured at the selected hex, not at an edge of the blast area. Sorties never finish off a unit: they soften, your troops finish. The enemy may have sorties too; get out of a marked area before it lands."
msgstr ""

#. [message]: speaker=narrator
#: utils/hte_macros.cfg:592
msgid "Veterans wear their rank. A unit that rises past its highest level gains a rank — Veteran, Seasoned veteran, Elite — shown as insignia on the unit: gold chevrons for the New Republic, the red-and-blue rank plaque for the Imperial Remnant, brass studs for independents, violet marks for dark Jedi. Promoted units such as sergeants and smuggler veterans start at the first rank. Many units and heroes also change their look as Seasoned veterans and Elites, earning heavier armour, capes and cloaks, or squadron colours. <b>Each rank also brings a new ability for the unit's class</b>: a sergeant's fire discipline, a medic's field surgery, a Wookiee's battle roar, a pilot's wingman call, an ace's marksman eye. They are listed at the end of each unit's description, so you can see what a veteran is working towards, and they are kept for good. No rank adds a new attack; abilities either work on their own or improve a weapon the unit already carries. At each rank a soldier, alien warrior, combat droid or starfighter chooses its training: +3 maximum hit points, a weapons drill (+1 damage on every weapon, up to twice) or a mobility drill (+1 movement, once). Every choice brings a full heal. Named heroes also choose <b>specializations</b> after their last level; the advancement dialog states each benefit and cap. Force discipline adds 2 maximum Force Points once (regeneration unchanged; ysalamiri still block the Force). Weapon disciplines add 1 damage per strike to the named weapon, up to twice, including its aimed shot where present. Combat maneuvering, blockade running or command repositioning adds 1 movement once, without extra vision. Infiltration lets Mara or Khabarakh ignore enemy zones of control. Field coordination restores up to 4 HP per turn to adjacent allies and slows poison; it never heals the commander or stacks with another healer. Every specialization fully heals, cures poison and slow, and raises the next XP requirement by 20%. Each hero retains the +3 maximum HP choice after all specialization caps."
msgstr ""

#. [message]: speaker=narrator
#: utils/hte_macros.cfg:593
msgid ""
"Grand Admiral Thrawn learns his enemies. His forces gain Insight from what they observe: how you move, the weapons you use, the targets you choose, your reinforcements, your formations and your tactics. Repeating yourself teaches him faster. Captured art, archives and intelligence can teach him too. Red pips over his commander show how far his doctrine has come.\n"
"\n"
"At set levels of Insight his forces gain, in order: an intelligence summary, pattern recognition, sensor doctrine (better identification and counter-jamming, and more decoys seen through), prediction of the units you are likely to attack next, and coordinated fire (+10% to hit the kinds of unit he has studied in action). Right-click his commander and choose <b>Tactical status</b> to see his current Insight and active effects. Insight never shows him what his sensors cannot detect, so cloaks and jamming still work against him. Vary your tactics."
msgstr ""

#. [message]: speaker=narrator
#: utils/hte_macros.cfg:596
msgid "Command decisions"
msgstr ""

#. [message]: speaker=narrator
#: utils/hte_macros.cfg:596
msgid "Once in each campaign — in Shadows of Kashyyyk, The Senator's Men and The Palace Infiltrators — High Command offers one kind of support for the rest of that campaign. <b>Supply convoy</b>: +25 credits at the start of every later mission where you recruit. <b>Veteran cadre</b>: two loyal units of your best recruit join at once, half-way to their next level. <b>Counter-intelligence</b>: Thrawn's doctrine never reaches Coordinated fire (its Insight stays at 75 or below). The choice stands until the campaign ends."
msgstr ""

#. [message]: speaker=narrator
#: utils/hte_macros.cfg:597
msgid "Achievements"
msgstr ""

#. [message]: speaker=narrator
#: utils/hte_macros.cfg:597
msgid ""
"Each campaign has achievements, listed under <b>Achievements</b> in the main menu: finish the campaign, finish it on Hard, finish it with no hero ever withdrawing, win missions with at least a third of their turns unused, become an ace by destroying enemy starfighters and warships in the space battles, and pull off feats in single missions: keep every warship at Thrawn's Gambit, every Dreadnaught at Battle for the Fleet, bring down the traitor at the Smugglers' Council, and break the line at The Last Command.\n"
"\n"
"The <i>Tactics</i> achievements reward the tools of this war, in any campaign: Force powers, overwatch, sensor sweeps and air support. Completing all three campaigns earns the last one."
msgstr ""

